WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.230
Now, let's talk about permission modes.

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:03.830
And when we say permission modes, it is

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.830
basically this toggle which you can find

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.710
near the chat window of your Claude

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:09.470
code. And if I click through here, you

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.510
can see here that there's multiple modes

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.470
here. There's ask before edits, there's

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.630
edit automatically, plan mode, auto

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:17.390
mode, and bypass permissions. And

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.470
basically what these modes do is they

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:21.790
just adjust how much control you can

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.110
give to Claude code in terms of viewing

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:25.870
and editing your files, which also

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:28.390
affects how fast it is that Claude code

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.030
can work. Because if we look at this

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:32.030
quick summary here, these permission

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:33.990
modes at the top actually give you more

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.710
control because you will find that you

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:37.990
will approve almost everything that

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.070
Claude code does before it edits stuff,

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.110
but it does compromise on speed. But if

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:43.750
you choose these permission modes at the

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:45.150
bottom, it does give you more speed

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.390
because there is less hand-holding and

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.270
less approvals that you need to give to

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:50.910
Claude code every time. But obviously,

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:53.150
there is a trade-off of lesser control.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:54.510
Now, I will actually show what each of

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:56.070
these permission modes does, but you can

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:57.630
see here that in a nutshell, what I'm

00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:59.390
recommending for most people is to

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.390
choose this auto mode permission. But

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:02.510
first, let's actually get you to have

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.310
visibility on auto mode and bypass

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:05.550
permissions. Because if you're just

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:06.870
starting Claude code for the first time,

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:08.750
what you would most likely see are just

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.070
these three modes. So, to get these

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:13.030
other two, what you just need to do once

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:14.950
is to enable them over at your Claude

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:16.550
code settings. So, to get them, you can

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.470
just go to settings here at the gear

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.190
icon at the bottom left, click on

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.030
settings, and if you search for Claude

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:23.550
code in here, then you should be able to

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:25.430
find this checkbox that lets you to

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.390
allow the bypass permission mode, which

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.590
is also known as dangerously skip

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:31.950
permissions. You can also the auto save

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:33.350
mode, which is actually the newest

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.030
amongst those five permission modes. So,

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.070
just go ahead and click that checkbox.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:38.630
So, once you exit this, you should now

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.870
have all of those five modes freely

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:42.710
available to you. Now, let's actually

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.430
look at these permission modes a bit

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:46.550
closer. And let's start with these first

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:47.750
two, the ones that are the most

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.430
restrictive amongst these five. You have

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.590
the ask before edits, and you have this

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.430
edit automatically. Now, with ask before

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:54.910
edits, that is the one that gives you

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:56.590
most control. So, Claude will ask for

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:59.070
approval before making each edit. If you

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:00.790
choose edit automatically, basically

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.510
what that will do is Claude it will edit

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.430
your selected text or the whole file

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.710
automatically. Now, that does seem like

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.029
it is a bit more permissive, but if you

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:10.790
run this simple command, let's say you

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.150
have this demo.md, which is just a text

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:14.670
file where it says "Dates added by

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:16.070
Claude will appear below." And then I

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:17.590
have a simple command here where I

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.310
wanted to add today's date to demo.md,

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:22.830
then open it in Notepad, which is an

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:25.510
application in my device. So, if I send

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.710
that, what's now going to happen is it's

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:29.910
going to read this file, so you can see

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:32.030
here that it is telling us that action

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.110
or tool call that it is doing at the

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.070
moment. And you can see it added today's

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:37.910
date, but when it comes to opening it in

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.550
the Notepad, it is asking for approval

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.670
to allow this bash command. And again,

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:45.510
bash is simply a terminal command that

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:47.270
allows Claude code to operate

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.110
applications on your behalf. And here

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:50.390
you can see there's the sort of

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.070
non-technical explanation to what it's

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:53.470
going to do, which is just to open

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:55.790
demo.md in Notepad. And from here, you

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:57.390
can choose the option whether you just

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.310
want to open that once, or if you choose

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:01.710
number two, then you will allow this

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:04.390
action for this project from here on

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.630
out. You can also just switch this so

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:09.509
that it only allows this action for this

00:03:09.519 --> 00:03:11.350
specific session, meaning this chat

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:12.830
session that you have at the moment. But

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:15.190
if you deem this to be a safe command,

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:16.630
then you can just go ahead and allow

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:18.670
that for the whole project and just

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:20.229
click that. And what that will do is

00:03:20.239 --> 00:03:22.670
open up that markdown file in Notepad,

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.270
which is what we instructed. But in

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:25.990
practice, now a lot of people actually

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:27.910
operate Claude code that way, especially

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:29.990
since now that we have auto mode, which

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.430
allows Claude code to be a bit more

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:33.670
smart around the decisions that it makes

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:34.949
on whether the stuff that it's going to

00:03:34.959 --> 00:03:36.870
do is actually destructive or not. At

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:38.790
least from my experience, I never saw

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:41.229
any instances from our business or from

00:03:41.239 --> 00:03:43.270
people whom I know where this auto mode

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:45.190
or even the bypass permissions where

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:47.190
Claude code runs destructive commands

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:48.910
without the user's permission. So, all

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:50.910
that to say that right now it is

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:53.230
relatively safe to just choose these

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:54.750
permission modes. But just to complete

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:56.470
the picture, if you want to see plan

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:58.190
mode in action, just go ahead and select

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:00.750
that. And let's say you have a longer

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:02.950
file and article here. If you have plan

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:04.990
mode selected, and you also have that

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:06.949
file in your view, which you can see

00:04:06.959 --> 00:04:09.110
here is selected by Claude code, which

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:10.590
by the way, if you click on that, you

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:12.590
can just toggle Claude code's visibility

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:14.230
on that file or not. Usually, I just

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:16.910
leave this on by default. If I ask it to

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:19.509
improve this article, let's say, then

00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:21.270
what is going to do is it's actually

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:23.990
going to present to me a proper plan

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.990
that I need to approve before it does

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:27.950
work on this article. And once it's

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:30.110
done, you can see that it opened up this

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:31.830
side panel for me, where it showed me

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:33.830
that plan, and I can either choose to

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.710
auto accept this plan, or manually

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:37.630
improve edits, or for it to continue

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:39.390
planning. So, if I hit yes and auto

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:41.270
accept there, what is now going to do is

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:43.790
just apply those changes to this article

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:46.190
as it sees fit. Now, the reason why this

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:48.790
mode is also not that popular is because

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:50.350
when you think about plan mode, you can

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:52.190
actually invoke this via natural

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:53.870
language as well. Meaning, if you talk

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:55.710
to Claude code and just ask it to plan

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:57.270
things out for you, then you actually

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:59.430
don't need to be in plan mode for it to

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:00.909
have a proper plan before doing

00:05:00.919 --> 00:05:02.630
anything. And so, usually, what I do,

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:04.430
and going back to the reason why I use

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:06.550
auto mode as default, is just have auto

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:08.790
mode here. And if I say to improve this

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:11.110
article, and just give me a plan, then

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:12.630
that's pretty much the same as using

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:14.590
plan mode, because as you can see here,

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:16.470
it just gives you the plan right within

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:18.430
the chat, which to me is a bit cleaner

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:21.150
to do, and it is also much easier to

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:23.550
provide feedback to it rather than that

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:25.710
interface that you saw using plan mode,

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:27.510
where it opened like a secondary sidebar

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:28.950
in order to do the same thing. Now,

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:30.550
lastly, before we close off with auto

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:32.909
mode, you can see bypass permissions is

00:05:32.919 --> 00:05:34.750
here, which actually, before auto mode

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:36.870
was launched a bit more recently, bypass

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:38.990
permissions is sort of the default for a

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:40.630
lot of people, including myself. In a

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:42.670
way, counterintuitive, because this is

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:44.670
also known as dangerously skips

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:46.310
permissions. But the thing with these

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:48.870
models that Anthropic has released is

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:50.710
that they are actually quite smart. So

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:52.430
even if you have bypass permissions as

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:54.990
on, usually it will still ask you for

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:57.790
permissions for any destructive commands

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:00.070
that it thinks to run. So we are at that

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:02.190
point where if you use these models,

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:03.790
then they have that knowledge not to

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:05.870
touch files that they don't need to or

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:08.030
even delete things that they know would

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:10.430
be bad for the user. But just to give an

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:12.070
example, let's say I have bypass

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:14.230
permissions as on and let's say we want

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:16.070
to improve this article, meaning this

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:18.150
one that we have on the left, then it

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:20.070
will actually just go ahead and do that

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:22.430
without asking for my permission to do

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:23.909
it. And so for most people, including

00:06:23.919 --> 00:06:25.390
myself, who have worked with these

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:27.270
agents for a couple of hundred hours

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:28.830
already, you will find at some point

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:30.470
that you will need to optimize for

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:32.630
speed. And so bypass permissions was my

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:34.590
go-to because that allows you to work

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:36.470
much faster as well. That said, if you

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:38.030
were using bypass permissions, you would

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:40.270
need to do a bit of setup in order to

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:41.830
just make sure that some destructive

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:44.150
commands are actually prohibited from

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:45.750
this workspace that you set up. And you

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:47.110
can obviously just ask Claude code to

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:49.710
set that up for you, but since auto mode

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:51.710
is now available, then I just generally

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:53.630
recommend people to use that. Because

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:55.310
auto mode, what it basically does is

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:57.110
that it actually lets Claude

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:59.270
automatically choose the best permission

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:01.150
mode for each task. And so if it thinks

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:02.950
that it's going to do something a bit

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:04.630
destructive, then it's actually going to

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:07.070
ask you that question. But if not, then

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:08.790
it will just go ahead and do things for

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:10.270
you, which is what we're using these

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:12.190
agents for anyway. Just to give a simple

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:14.190
example, let's say you send a prompt

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:16.430
like this where you wanted to delete

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:18.150
your whole workspace. So obviously, that

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:20.030
is something that it would need to ask

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:22.150
your permission to do. So you can see

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:24.270
here that it gave us a note to hold on.

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:25.270
I want to make sure before doing

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:26.350
anything here because this is

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:28.430
destructive and not reversible. So it's

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:30.430
not just going to do that deletion on

00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:32.270
its own. What it's going to do is to

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:33.790
actually talk to you and to just make

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:35.550
sure that it got your instructions

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:37.750
correctly. And so in a nutshell, this

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:39.950
permission modes piece, it is just good

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:42.630
to know what they are, but generally for

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:44.750
most people, I would just suggest to use

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:47.270
auto mode every time because that is

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:49.390
going to give you the right balance of

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:53.440
speed and control.
